		)
		(uuid "f4159c82-1649-9d8e-295a-4b1cdc3f211c")
	)
	(text "RASPBERRY PI 40-PIN HEADER"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 15.24 20.32 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 3.6 3.6)
				(bold yes)
			)
			(justify left bottom)
		)
		(uuid "f43a1887-4bb6-36d2-b106-8447a1f795be")
	)
	(text "W6   IO_L22N_T3_13   unit 8 pps"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 346.71 86.36 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.2 1.2)
				(italic yes)
			)
			(justify left bottom)
		)
		(uuid "f65489e7-88cb-5bf6-d23c-bb2ccd7273a0")
	)
	(text "Y17   IO_L7N_T1_34   unit 3 pps"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 346.71 101.6 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.2 1.2)
				(italic yes)
			)
			(justify left bottom)
		)
		(uuid "f9f329e0-9bba-7efb-cb7d-668809c0331d")
	)
	(text_box "NO MODIFICATION TO THE PYNQ-Z2 IS NEEDED - BUT PMOD A BECOMES UNUSABLE.\nThis board is designed for a stock PYNQ-Z2.  It relies on the 200 ohm series resistor the PYNQ puts in every\nRaspberry Pi GPIO, and on the 2k2 I2C pull-ups above; both are fitted from the factory and neither should be\nremoved.\nWhat it does take is the WHOLE header.  The PYNQ-Z2 user manual, section 1: 'Raspberry Pi connector - 28 Total\nFPGA I/O (8 pins are shared with Pmod A)'.  This design uses all 28, so all eight of Pmod A's I/O land on pins\nwe drive: JA1P/JA1N = BEAT_01/BEAT_04, JA2P/JA2N = BEAT_02/PPS_02, JA3P/JA3N = BEAT_06/PPS_00, and JA4P/JA4N =\nthe I2C bus itself.  Plugging anything into Pmod A puts a second driver on those FPGA balls.  Verified ball by\nball against the manual's Pmod tables and against the TUL schematic's own SHARE PIN reference table: Pmod B\nand all three Arduino headers share nothing with us and stay fully usable.\nPOWER: this board draws about 110 mA from the header's 5 V, on top of whatever the Zynq is already taking.\nThe manual says the micro-USB port 'should provide enough power for most designs' - use the barrel jack with\nJ9 = REG instead."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 15.24 232.41 0)
		(size 251.46 36.83)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 255 220 220 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "0179a625-655f-6121-2dfa-5874f5833038")
	)
	(text_box "THE ROW SPLIT IS NOT A COINCIDENCE.\nAfter taking pins 3 and 5 for I2C the odd row has exactly 13 signal pins left,\nand after taking pin 18 for the helper the even row has exactly 12.  So every\nBEAT is on the odd row and every PPS on the even row, which separates the two\nfamilies across the connector and makes the board-side fan-out a clean two-bus\nlayout."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 274.32 139.7 0)
		(size 121.92 17.78)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 220 235 255 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "1fc3a908-85a4-ee9a-e693-d30349efad03")
	)
	(text_box "THE HELPER GOES IN ON PIN 18.\nY7 = rpio_24 = IO_L13P_T2_MRCC_13: an MRCC P-side pin in bank 13, the best clock\ninput on the board.  The same physical pin is a dedicated PCLKT6_1 primary clock\ninput on an Icepi Zero, so one choice serves both platforms.  Pin 31 (U18, MRCC\nbank 34) is the fallback."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 144.78 139.7 0)
		(size 121.92 15.24)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 220 235 255 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "40deaad9-e96c-6ee6-7c57-5783c30eb2cd")
	)
	(text_box "PIN 1 AND PIN 17 ARE LEFT UNCONNECTED.\nBoth are the host's 3.3 V.  We make our own 3.3 V from the header's 5 V, and not\nbridging to the host's rail means we cannot back-feed it.\nThe TUL user manual's Table 22 shows pin 1 as GROUND; the schematic shows 3V3,\nand the schematic is right.  Leaving the pin open makes the question moot either\nway."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 15.24 139.7 0)
		(size 121.92 17.78)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 255 220 220 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "8fe16247-4255-5dc6-d97d-a3d02c92b48f")
	)
	(text_box "PIN 13 IS REAL AND IS MISSING FROM THE STOCK XDC.\nNet RPIO_27_R, package pin V7, IO_L11N_T1_SRCC_13, fed through R87 (200R 1%).\nThe widely-circulated pynq-z2.xdc simply omits it, so anyone counting from that\nfile finds 26 usable pins where the board has 28.  On this board it carries\nBEAT_05.  Add the line by hand:\nset_property -dict {PACKAGE_PIN V7 IOSTANDARD LVCMOS33} [get_ports {beat[05]}]"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 403.86 139.7 0)
		(size 121.92 17.78)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 255 245 210 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "ac10a53c-6a38-d3c2-e918-34866edd8126")
	)
	(text_box "2k2 PULL-UPS ARE ALREADY FITTED ON THE PYNQ-Z2 on header pins 3, 5, 27 and 28 (R305-R308), which is the\nstandard Raspberry Pi I2C arrangement.  Do not add more on this board.  Pins 27 and 28 carry BEAT_02 and\nPPS_02; a 2k2 pull-up on a push-pull LVC output is 1.5 mA and harmless, and any rise/fall asymmetry it causes\nis a per-channel constant that the null test removes."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 15.24 185.42 0)
		(size 251.46 12.7)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 255 245 210 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "e48e633b-2d81-ddb2-eac2-ee65241a5614")
	)
